From 520e405d0aca225d9180ae31b017bfeb2da7b576 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 02:26:01 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 16/25] Add a VSX implementation of the 2D filter SIMD
 primitives on PowerPC

The SVG/CSS filter code in gfx/2d (blending, colour matrix, morphology,
composition, opacity, turbulence, arithmetic combine, alpha extraction and
format conversion) is written against the small SIMD abstraction in
SIMD.h, which had a scalar and an SSE2 implementation. This adds a VSX
implementation under SIMD_COMPILE_VSX, with distinct vector types for the
8-, 16- and 32-bit integer and float lanes, and FilterProcessingVSX.cpp
instantiates the filter templates with them. FilterProcessing dispatches
to it through supports_vsx().

Lane order follows memory order on both endiannesses; only the zero
extension of bytes to 16-bit lanes needs an endian switch. The widening
multiplies use vec_mule/vec_mulo plus merges, the pairwise multiply-add is
vec_msum, and the saturating packs follow the scalar reference semantics
(32ToU16 clamps to [0, INT16_MAX], F32ToI32 is floor(x + 0.5)). The
blending code shuffles 32-bit lanes of a 16-bit-lane vector, so Shuffle32
gets an overload for that type.
